          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            may seem to be a topic curiously disconnected from the core of mathematical practice. But in fact, the foundations of mathematics has a significant impact upon core mathematics; different
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundational+axiom"} foundational rules and axioms
            included in the foundations of mathematics would result in certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
            being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            in one foundation of mathematics,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
            in another foundation of mathematics, and
            %span.newWikiWord
              independent
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/independent"} ?
            in a third foundation of mathematics. This page lists numerous examples where questions of foundations do affect questions and problems in core mathematics.
          %p
            The term “core mathematics” is discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Quinn12"} Quinn 2012
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#LargeCardinalAxiom Large cardinal axioms
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal+axiom"}> Large cardinal axioms
            , traditionally not assumed in various foundations, have effects in real mathematics:
          %ul
            %li
              That every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extremally+disconnected"} extremally disconnected
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+set"} profinite set
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercover"} hypercover
              whose terms are extremally disconnected requires that beth cardinals exist. See Peter Scholze’s comment
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2021/06/large_sets_2.html#c060032"} here
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %h3#excluded_middle Excluded middle
          %ul
            %li
              That every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact space
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subnet"} subnet
              that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/converges"} converges
              is equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %h3#axiom_of_choice Axiom of choice
          %p
            See the nLab’s
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice#equivalents"} list of statements equivalent to the axiom of choice
          %p
            See also Wikipedia’s
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice#Equivalents"} list of statements equivalent to the axiom of choice
          %p
            There are a number of theorems that are strictly weaker than the axiom of choice but traditionally proved using the axiom of choice; these could be assumed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundational+axioms"} foundational axioms
            of intermediate strength between nothing and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice#Weaker_forms"} here
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "http://consequences.emich.edu/conseq.htm"} Consequences of the Axiom of Choice Project
            provides an interactive data base that can be used to search for implications between various (weakened) forms of the Axiom of Choice.
            %a{:href => "http://cgraph.inters.co/"} Choiceless grapher
            builds on this data and provides a graphical presentation.
          %p
            See also Wikipedia's
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_statements_undecidable_in_ZFC"} list of statements undecidable in ZFC
          %h3#whiteheads_theorem Whitehead’s theorem
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead%27s+theorem"} Whitehead's theorem
            is a theorem valid for all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"} infinity-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercomplete+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} hypercomplete (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundational+axiom"} foundational axiom
            that may be regarded as a “classicality” property, akin to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            or the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %em Whitehead’s principle
            by homotopy type theorists.
          %h3#whitehead_problem Whitehead problem
          %p
            The following question is called the
            %strong Whitehead problem
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+abelian+group"} free abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            satisfies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ext
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext^1(A,\mathbb{Z}) = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            that satisfies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ext
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext^1(A,\mathbb{Z}) = 0
            a free abelian group?
          %p
            When formalizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+theory"} group theory
            within
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h3#fermats_last_theorem Fermat’s last theorem
          %p
            It was
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elementary_function_arithmetic#Friedman.27s_grand_conjecture"} conjectured
            by Harvey Friedman that all theorems involving finitary objects published in
            %em Annals of Mathematics
            (for argument’s sake) can be proved in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elementary+Function+Arithmetic"} Elementary Function Arithmetic
            (EFA), a weak fragment of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat%27s+last+theorem"} Fermat's last theorem
            is provable in PA. There is a current program of research by Angus MacIntyre to show this last fact directly. From a category theoretic point of view,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
            has shown that all of the material in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
            relies only on quite weak foundations, namely MacLane set theory (
            %a{:href => "#McLarty20"}> McLarty 2020
            ) with one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            (weaker than the theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{\omega\cdot 3}
            considered in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %p
            %a{:href => "#McLarty10"} McLarty 2010
            comments extensively on the possibility of proving Fermat’s last theorem, and more generally the Modularity theorem, in PA.
          %h3#busy_beaver_function Busy Beaver function
          %p
            The value of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Busy+Beaver+function"} Busy Beaver function
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 748
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 748
            (in fact already at
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 745
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 745
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            axioms (
            %a{:href => "busy+beaver+function#Riebel23"}> Riebel 2023
            , recalled by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "busy+beaver+function#Aaronson23"} Aaronson 2023
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 549
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 549
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "busy+beaver+function#BBCWiki"} the Busy Beaver Challenge wiki
          %p
            %a{:href => "busy+beaver+function#Aaronson20"} Aaronson 2020
            has conjectured that the value of the Busy Beaver function is independent of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            axioms at
